  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 253 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nSimilar to Wednesday, whatever showers and storms early Thursday \nlook to become less common through the day as forcing weakens. \nThis will be in part as the ridge over the southeastern US \nbulges a bit poleward, which pushes the upper level kinematics \nand track of the nebulous vorticity piece away from Iowa. \nFurther, low pressure over the High Plains look to surge the \nelevated warm front well north of the state with the highest \nrainfall amounts now trending northward to northern Iowa if not \npoints farther north over South Dakota and Minnesota. This focus\nof rainfall may remain north Friday night into Saturday. \nMeanwhile, a substantive trough over the interior West will push\neastward on Friday, which will finally shunt the ridge\nsoutheastward, but also bring the return of favorable heavy \nrainfall parameters this weekend. This front should bring drier \nconditions at some point, eventually, next week in what is an \noverall wet and active pattern until then.",
